    {
      "id": "be-s7-budget-constraint-q-1f02fb46",
      "type": "long",
      "stem": "Explain the budget constraint: opportunity set, budget line, intercepts, slope and the effect of changes in income and prices.",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "**Introduction.** The budget constraint shows what bundles of X and Y a consumer can afford.\n\n**Opportunity set.** Affordable bundles: PxX + PyY ≤ M.\n\n**Budget line.** Bundles that exhaust income: PxX + PyY = M, or Y = M/Py - (Px/Py)X. Intercepts: M/Py on the Y axis and M/Px on the X axis.\n\n**Slope.** Px/Py is the market rate of substitution.\n\n**Income changes.** Increases shift the line outward in parallel (M1 > M0); decreases shift it downward in parallel (M2 < M0).\n\n**Price changes.** A fall in the price of X rotates the line counter-clockwise; a rise rotates it clockwise.\n\n**Conclusion.** Income moves the line; prices tilt it.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "point": "Opportunity set and budget line equations",
          "source_ref": "be--session-7#10"
        },
        {
          "point": "Intercepts and slope",
          "source_ref": "be--session-7#10"
        },
        {
          "point": "Income changes shift in parallel",
          "source_ref": "be--session-7#11"
        },
        {
          "point": "Price changes rotate",
          "source_ref": "be--session-7#11"
        },
        {
          "point": "Market rate of substitution",
          "source_ref": "be--session-7#10"
        }
      ],
      "marks": 5.0,
      "difficulty": 2,
      "style_tag": "explain",
      "source_refs": [
        "be--session-7#10",
        "be--session-7#11"
      ],
      "derived": [],
      "needs_check": false,
      "retired": false
    },
    {
      "id": "be-s7-budget-constraint-q-98661ff0",
      "type": "long",
      "stem": "The price of good X falls while income and the price of Y stay the same. Explain, using the budget line equation, how the line changes and what this does to the opportunity set.",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "**Introduction.** The budget line is Y = M/Py - (Px/Py)X, so a lower Px changes the slope but not the Y intercept.\n\n**Y intercept.** M/Py is unchanged, because neither M nor Py changed.\n\n**X intercept.** M/Px grows: the line reaches further along the X axis (from M0/Px0 to M0/Px1, with Px0 > Px1).\n\n**Slope.** Px/Py gets smaller, so the line gets flatter: a counter-clockwise rotation.\n\n**Opportunity set.** The set of affordable bundles expands.\n\n**Conclusion.** A price fall pivots the line outward around the Y intercept.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "point": "Y intercept M/Py unchanged",
          "source_ref": "be--session-7#10"
        },
        {
          "point": "X intercept moves out",
          "source_ref": "be--session-7#11"
        },
        {
          "point": "Counter-clockwise rotation, flatter slope",
          "source_ref": "be--session-7#11"
        },
        {
          "point": "Opportunity set expands",
          "source_ref": "be--session-7#11"
        }
      ],
      "marks": 5.0,
      "difficulty": 3,
      "style_tag": "explain",
      "source_refs": [
        "be--session-7#10",
        "be--session-7#11"
      ],
